#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180639} :activity/id #uuid "39af6225-7719-5f09-ae8c-6df89ff6b7cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048280 :topic/id #uuid "900f65f4-ad2b-51fa-b027-f2b2cba3065b" :topic/math-academy-id 3754} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180661 :tutorial/content "If we start with the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} ,\n$$\n\nwe can use the relationship between multiplication and division to write down a formula to calculate a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how to apply this formula using a real-world example.\n\nSuppose that $54$ out of $300$ boxes of grapes in a supermarket contain red grapes. What percentage of the boxes contain red grapes?\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of red grapes, which is $54$, and the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $300.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 54 \\div 300 = \\dfrac{54}{300}.\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{54}{300} = \\dfrac{54 \\div 3}{300 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{18}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{100} = 18 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\%$ of the boxes contain red grapes." :tutorial/id #uuid "73bca8be-ae46-5214-9ba6-2beb553be49f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2082 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bdf65be2-4038-5c62-b8e0-2362d10939cc" :step/math-academy-id 7964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179496 :question/id #uuid "75ac9866-6b0f-5736-a592-0bade16b8814" :question/math-academy-id "e-2333"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050776}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538994}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"} :step/id #uuid "2a3797e0-441d-5d87-adba-4fa2a33a82b4" :step/math-academy-id 7965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179497 :question/id #uuid "2f3270cc-d191-5282-b9f7-cf63dcd69d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-2334"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459309}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification"} :step/id #uuid "d0c640ea-bd66-5449-a695-d9913915d65f" :step/math-academy-id 7966}] :activity/title "Finding a Percentage Given Two Numbers: Word Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179897}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139294} :activity/id #uuid "88d9417f-4dcb-5b07-99a4-4fc3631a5617" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438 :topic/id #uuid "9f62517d-bdce-5662-9e1b-19d15df5109b" :topic/math-academy-id 2234} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139715 :tutorial/content "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d44fcde5-ad8a-596b-a6ac-955ff40b6bd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8751 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc0c262d-dd9e-52a1-a83b-9f1c0fe96262" :step/math-academy-id 165169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139716 :tutorial/content "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1808067-d542-577c-a2d3-085e0fb1df59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8752 :tutorial/title "The Greatest Possible Error"} :step/id #uuid "66ba1355-ab27-5d81-8157-a359b9801285" :step/math-academy-id 165170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138164 :question/id #uuid "b18fef84-bbbb-58c6-a215-4146461c28f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050051}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476950}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "bf5aa060-ae70-531a-8e1c-d165c1d25adb" :step/math-academy-id 165171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138165 :question/id #uuid "9e0f0485-0635-558e-8493-eef61ee623f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14085"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050934}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536858}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place"} :step/id #uuid "7d898aa1-1cb4-5977-87b3-02ae7082146a" :step/math-academy-id 165174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138167 :question/id #uuid "44ff13ac-1980-554e-aea8-4aaa51d202fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050792}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539011}] :knowledge-point/title "Bounding Areas and Volumes"} :step/id #uuid "761a41b0-39e8-54bb-9403-886bbe9ed521" :step/math-academy-id 165177}] :activity/title "Degrees of Accuracy" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138593}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539064} :activity/id #uuid "b2fa5316-e98c-5047-902c-0d6c438a9d06" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438 :topic/id #uuid "9f62517d-bdce-5662-9e1b-19d15df5109b" :topic/math-academy-id 2234} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255bade8-0a89-5de2-8a24-9fd1e2100b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538308} {:answer/id #uuid "31e67d7e-4e60-5f06-8489-9ee7a57c5092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.7\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538335} {:answer/id #uuid "995c6b46-9527-5d81-9c6e-1f7397ab9bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538593} {:answer/id #uuid "a64f0aa8-5dbd-5dd3-ae18-7c76124f8c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538631} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fd67f7-1c22-5e81-be9b-2698dfa44075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.5\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538631} :answer-field/id #uuid "278391dd-df9b-5ab8-b856-03ca04c5de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cc1f868e-acd2-53b5-a61f-6b065116ee9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-46386" :question/problem "The dimensions of a cubic water tank are $2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m},$  where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, what is the smallest possible volume of the tank?" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible volume of the tank. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible volume of the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =12.977\\,875\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter."} :step/id #uuid "59367d0f-feb4-57b0-bee3-c4cdea97ec6b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2acee517-33dc-5f55-a0b3-f02e6c6263fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538323} :answer-field/id #uuid "62968f0c-410e-58a3-b547-3822b3d5961c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a0cc887e-fde1-561f-a2a2-0e9220220d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-210524" :question/problem "The dimensions of a rectangular floor are $12\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest square meter, what is the smallest possible area of the floor?\n\nTo the nearest square meter, the smallest possible area is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the container. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=11.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 3\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=2.5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible area of the container is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =11.5\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =28.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈29\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square meter."} :step/id #uuid "67e2fe74-1f9c-5f39-b367-3beb48c1832e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16fac51a-9457-5261-b22b-f89d79af0f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.09\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538282} {:answer/id #uuid "2e710c8a-03cf-5964-8f07-e6abecb91318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.0\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538329} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4403cc-a3af-50f8-91d2-7e006f9ca2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.095\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.105\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538391} {:answer/id #uuid "61ad7ba7-c6e6-5a77-88a2-8f331b4e6cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538448} {:answer/id #uuid "de59b963-7146-502e-8106-8eeb734cdb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.1\\,\\text{m}\\le h<3.1\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538448} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b01a9c5-17a4-5cf7-9eed-2c1fcee823f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66b93aa5-a873-551c-b039-7debf6d7af79" :question/math-academy-id "q-154080" :question/problem "The shoulder height of a moose $h$ measures $2.1\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest tenth of a meter. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "afdc7c1a-9e06-55c0-8af8-0603e546f8f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539066}}] :activity/title "Degrees of Accuracy" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538226}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055521} :activity/id #uuid "5844d9da-308a-5428-8509-e1a619cc5407" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048594 :topic/id #uuid "acd6ee8f-99a9-566d-947a-0a7133b158e6" :topic/math-academy-id 121} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056019 :tutorial/content "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.  First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9}  = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we multiply:\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times 9 = -18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70914b55-4296-53ed-8b9b-443bffeea358" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "88234dee-6508-5596-8e07-fe4c5d34c2cd" :step/math-academy-id 132145 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054606 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054396 :question/id #uuid "6ce1d2ce-e99a-5f5c-a698-3f744805ef24" :question/math-academy-id "e-12921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdadd355-0ce5-598a-b9cd-f189ccfb7ca6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480662}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Stand-Alone Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "79808ac9-8172-551f-a71d-9565ab867f05" :step/math-academy-id 132146 :st